  "version": "1.0.3",
  "notes": "Real hardware run of modem_power_cycle_test.py (1.0.2, with the corrected AT+CPOF command) gave a definitive answer this time: AT+CPOF returned OK (genuinely powered the module off), and afterward plain AT got '(no response)' even after the normal reset() sequence -- confirmed FAIL. config.py's PIN_MODEM_RESET is a plain RESET line on this board, not the module's PWRKEY, and cannot wake it from a true power-off; recovering required a physical power cycle. AT+CPOF-based power-down is therefore NOT safe to wire into the automatic logging flow without either rewiring to the module's actual PWRKEY pin, or accepting a physical power cycle every time. Added modem_cfun_test.py instead: tests AT+CFUN=0 (minimum functionality -- RF/network circuits off, but the module's own AT manual confirms it stays attached to the UART/AT interface the whole time, no PWRKEY needed at all) followed by AT+CFUN=1 to restore full functionality and re-register. Saves less power than a true power-off would (baseband + UART stay powered), but has none of AT+CPOF's wake-up risk on this board's wiring.",
